.TH RESMAN 8 "September 2026" "v1.35.2" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
resman \- Dynamic CPU/RAM/IO resource manager for Linux using cgroups v2
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B resman
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B resman
is a daemon that monitors system and per\-user CPU, RAM, and block I/O usage.
When configured resource thresholds are exceeded, it dynamically applies CPU
quota and weight, memory high and maximum limits, and I/O bandwidth and IOPS
limits to selected users. It uses the Linux cgroups v2 CPU, memory, and I/O
controllers to enforce those limits.
.PP
The daemon operates in control cycles configured via
.B POLLING_INTERVAL
and makes decisions based on configurable CPU, RAM, and I/O thresholds to
activate, maintain, or remove resource limits.
.SH FEATURES
.IP \(bu 2
Dynamic CPU limiting based on configurable thresholds
.IP \(bu
Dynamic RAM limiting with soft/hard limits (memory.high/memory.max)
.IP \(bu
IO bandwidth and IOPS limiting (io.max)
.IP \(bu
Automatic user detection with authoritative systemd unit management
.IP \(bu
Blackout timeframes support (BLACKOUT)
.IP \(bu
User filtering with regex support (USER_INCLUDE_LIST, USER_EXCLUDE_LIST)
.IP \(bu
Process exclusion list (PROCESS_EXCLUDE_LIST). Excluded processes remain in
observed totals but do not contribute to CPU, RAM, or I/O decisions and are not
moved into limited cgroups. Matching uses only the /proc/PID/exe basename;
/proc/PID/comm is display-only because a process can rewrite it. If executable
identity is unavailable, the process remains in observation and ResMan reports an
explicit error. ResMan never moves a process; systemd-native limits apply to the
authoritative user slice only when the complete resource authority is known.
.IP \(bu
Threshold time window to prevent false activations (CPU_THRESHOLD_DURATION)
.IP \(bu
Username resolution cache for improved performance (USERNAME_CACHE_TTL)
.IP \(bu
SQLite metrics database for historical data (METRICS_DB_*)
.IP \(bu
MCP server with a mechanically verified tool inventory for AI assistant integration
.IP \(bu
Prometheus metrics export with hostname and server_role labels
.IP \(bu
LDAP/NIS username resolution support (requires CGO)
.IP \(bu
Workload pattern detection for adaptive limits (AUTODETECT_PATTERNS)
.IP \(bu
Separate timeout configuration for the complete daemon shutdown sequence
(DAEMON_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT) and the MCP server shutdown step
(MCP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT)
.IP \(bu
PSI event-driven control cycles for reduced polling overhead (PSI_EVENT_DRIVEN)
.IP \(bu
Performance optimizations: gopsutil process scanning and regex cache
.IP \(bu
Graceful shutdown with proper resource cleanup
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR
Specifies the configuration file path. Default:
.I /etc/resman/resman.conf
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Displays program version and exits.
.SH PLATFORM REQUIREMENTS
.B resman
requires Linux kernel 4.18 or later with cgroups v2 enabled.
.PP
At startup, resman probes a real child cgroup for the interfaces required by the
configured features. CPU Points enforcement requires both
.B cpu.max
and
.BR cpu.weight .
When
.B RAM_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
is configured,
.B memory.max
is mandatory; when
.B IO_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
is configured,
.B io.max
is mandatory. A controller name in
.B cgroup.controllers
is not sufficient. A missing required interface causes startup to fail with the
feature, controller, and interface named. The
.B cpuset
controller is optional for CPU Points quota and proportional-weight enforcement.
.PP
Capability discovery is a startup snapshot. If
.B memory.max
or
.B io.max
was unusable during that probe, a later reload that requests the corresponding
feature reports the capability error and publishes the effective configuration with
the feature still disabled. Fixing the host does not refresh the snapshot: restart
resman to discover the repaired interface. By contrast, a feature that was merely
disabled in the startup configuration can be enabled by reload without a restart when
its interface was already found usable; failure to establish authoritative systemd
resource control is reported and leaves the feature disabled.
.PP
The published RPM is built with CGO on Enterprise Linux 8 and targets the EL8
userspace ABI (glibc 2.28). It is intended for EL8 and later compatible
Enterprise Linux releases. The RPM is not statically linked because NSS, LDAP,
NIS, and SSSD username resolution require CGO and the system C library.
.PP
PSI event\-driven operation additionally requires kernel PSI support. On
Enterprise Linux systems where PSI is disabled at boot, enable it with
.BR psi=1
as described in the PSI section of this manual.
.SH SYSTEMD RESTART CONTRACT
The packaged systemd unit distinguishes failures that require operator action from
failures that may be transient. Invalid configuration, unusable cgroup capabilities
required by an enabled feature, and invalid MCP TLS credentials exit with status
.BR 78 .
The unit uses the systemd notification protocol and reports readiness only after
configuration, capability, and configured listener initialization have completed.
Consequently,
.B systemctl start resman
waits for that boundary and fails when startup is rejected instead of reporting an
optimistic success for a process that immediately exits. The unit reports the service
as failed and does not restart it automatically. Inspect
.BR "systemctl status resman"
and
.BR "journalctl -u resman" ,
correct the configuration or host capability, then start the service again.
.PP
An error reading or writing setup state for an otherwise present cgroup capability,
including a failed write to
.BR cgroup.subtree_control ,
is treated as potentially transient rather than as proof that the capability is
structurally absent. These and other non-zero exits are retried after 10 seconds. The
unit permits at most three
starts within 60 seconds; after that it remains failed with a start-limit result rather
than retrying forever. After correcting the transient cause, run
.B systemctl reset-failed resman
before starting the service when the start limit was reached. A clean stop is not
automatically restarted.
.SH ARCHITECTURE AND CPU ALLOCATION
Current metrics schema: 7.
.PP
The system normalizes current online CPU capacity to 1000 CPU Points and uses a
flat systemd-owned hierarchy. The finite parent user.slice contains active user
slices, with dedicated root entitlement, exact mapped weights and one aggregate
best-effort allocation partitioned among unmapped and excluded users.
Unused capacity is available to all runnable siblings. No PID leaves its session
or service unit. CPU covers rootless descendants within the user's slice.
.PP
The map named by
.B CPU_POINTS_FILE
must begin with
.B [resman-cpu-points-map-v1]
and then contain exact
.I username=points
assignments. The marker must be the first physical line. UTF-8 with LF or CRLF is
accepted without a BOM; empty lines and unindented full-line comments are allowed;
an assignment has exactly one equals sign, no surrounding whitespace, and canonical
decimal points from 1 through 1000. The complete username is passed byte-for-byte to
NSS. Thus
.B john.smith=200
names the exact dotted identity rather than a nested key. Duplicate names, duplicate
resolved UIDs, ambiguous or non-exact NSS results, unsafe ancestors, symbolic links,
non-regular files, wrong ownership, or a mode other than 0600 reject the complete
snapshot. The packaged default is the root-owned regular file
.B /etc/resman/cpu-points.map
below root-owned mode-0700
.BR /etc/resman .
Configured guarantees plus CPU_ROOT_POINTS and CPU_BEST_EFFORT_POINTS must not
exceed 1000 minus CPU_RESERVE_POINTS. Root login sessions stay inside the finite
pool. The reserve protects system.slice and other workloads outside user.slice, not an unbounded root shell.
.PP
Defaults are CPU_RESERVE_POINTS=100, CPU_ROOT_POINTS=100 and
CPU_BEST_EFFORT_POINTS=100, leaving at most 700 mapped points. The old default-budget
range 701-800 is rejected; see the 750-point rebalance example in UPGRADING.md.
Root has a lendable scheduling entitlement, not a ceiling: ResMan never writes CPUQuota on user-0.slice.
.PP
Let M be the largest configured entitlement among root, aggregate best effort and
all mapped guarantees. The exact scale is floor(10000 / M). Active best-effort slices
must not exceed CPU_BEST_EFFORT_POINTS * floor(10000 / M): 10000 with the empty default
map, or 1400 with a 700-point guarantee and best effort 100. Their individual weights
differ by at most one and sum exactly to the aggregate. An impossible plan fails
before mutation with best_effort_cardinality and its active count, aggregate weight
and maximum scale. This is a topology bound, not a per-user guarantee.
.PP
Changes to the reserve, root, best-effort entitlement, or map contents are applied as
one composite hot-reload epoch. Both files are confirmed before and after kernel
reconciliation. Changing
.B CPU_POINTS_FILE
itself requires a restart; a rejected path change never redirects the live reload.
Active class and weight changes reconcile in place with verified acknowledgement.
.PP
CPU Points observation uses the same decision interval for parent and per-slice
usage deltas. See docs/CPU-POINTS-OBSERVABILITY.md for schema 7, independent resource
coverage and the 60-second measurement procedure. Ratios use effective parent
delivery and confirmed sibling weights, not nominal quota as a delivered guarantee.
CPU Points are scheduling entitlements implemented as weights;
realized share depends on thread placement. Measure actual delivery counters,
not an assumed algebraic floor. A controlled-placement gate PASS does not establish
the same precision for unbound workloads; its tolerances are not operator thresholds.
.PP
CPU limit eligibility is controlled by
.B USER_INCLUDE_LIST
and
.BR USER_EXCLUDE_LIST .
.PP
For every regex-list key, a comma is the list separator and cannot occur inside
one pattern. This applies to CPU, RAM and I/O user lists and to
.BR PROCESS_EXCLUDE_LIST .
A bounded quantifier such as
.B {2,4}
is therefore rejected. Rewrite it without a comma, for example by expanding it
into comma-free alternatives such as
.BR "(?:x{2}|x{3}|x{4})" .
An empty or unset
.B USER_INCLUDE_LIST
keeps metrics monitoring active but makes no user eligible for CPU limits.
Set
.B USER_INCLUDE_LIST=.*
to make every non\-excluded user eligible. This fail\-safe CPU behavior does
not change the independent RAM and I/O include lists, where an empty list
continues to include all users.
.SH RAM LIMITS
When enabled, the daemon monitors total memory usage by non\-system users and applies limits when the configured threshold is exceeded.
.PP
RAM limiting uses cgroups v2 memory controller with two levels of control:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B memory.max
- hard accounting boundary at which the kernel attempts reclaim and may invoke cgroup OOM
.IP \(bu
.B memory.high
- soft limit (memory pressure triggers reclaim, processes may throttle)
.RE
.PP
Configuration parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
.B RAM_LIMIT_ENABLED
- Enable RAM limiting (true/false)
.IP \(bu
.B RAM_THRESHOLD
- Activation threshold percentage of total user RAM usage
.IP \(bu
.B RAM_RELEASE_THRESHOLD
- Deactivation threshold percentage
.IP \(bu
.B RAM_QUOTA_PER_USER
- Per\-user RAM limit (when limits active)
.IP \(bu
.B DISABLE_SWAP
- Set memory.swap.max=0 to prevent swap usage (true/false)
.IP \(bu
.B RAM_HIGH_RATIO
- Ratio between memory.high and memory.max (0.0\-1.0, default 0.8)
.IP \(bu
.B RAM_USER_INCLUDE_LIST
- Regex patterns for users subject to RAM limits (empty = all)
.IP \(bu
.B RAM_USER_EXCLUDE_LIST
- Regex patterns for users excluded from RAM limits
.PP
When RAM limits are active, each user's cgroup receives both
.B memory.max
and
.B memory.high
limits according to the configured quotas and ratio.
.PP
Native memory limits apply to existing slice charges after independent authority
validation. No resource transition changes process membership or the accounting
identity.
.PP
The normal composition places
.B memory.high
below
.BR memory.max .
Without swap or reclaimable pages, a process can remain alive but make negligible
progress at high indefinitely: the memory-current value plateaus below max, high
events continue rising, and max, OOM, OOM-kill and OOM-group-kill counters remain zero.
Requested allocation size alone therefore does not promise termination. Raise or
disable high, provide reclaimable capacity or swap, or release the RAM limit. An
explicit high-equals-max control is a distinct diagnostic configuration that can
exercise max/OOM behavior without the lower high boundary stalling progress first.
.PP
RAM or I/O eligibility never creates a standalone cgroup and never changes CPU
membership. Properties are applied only to an authoritative user slice with complete
resource authority.
.SH IO LIMITS
When enabled, the daemon monitors I/O usage and applies bandwidth and IOPS limits via cgroups v2 io controller.
.PP
IO limiting supports both bandwidth (bytes per second) and IOPS (operations per second) limits for read and write operations independently.
.PP
Configuration parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
.B IO_LIMIT_ENABLED
- Enable IO limiting (true/false)
.IP \(bu
.B IO_THRESHOLD
- Activation percentage applied independently to each configured I/O dimension;
  any dimension at or above the threshold activates or maintains enforcement
.IP \(bu
.B IO_RELEASE_THRESHOLD
- Release percentage; every configured I/O dimension must be below it
.IP \(bu
.B IO_READ_BPS
- Read bandwidth limit (e.g., "100M", "1G")
.IP \(bu
.B IO_WRITE_BPS
- Write bandwidth limit
.IP \(bu
.B IO_READ_IOPS
- Read IOPS limit (0 = unlimited)
.IP \(bu
.B IO_WRITE_IOPS
- Write IOPS limit (0 = unlimited)
.PP
Bandwidth values of "max" or an empty value and IOPS values of 0 disable only
their own dimension. Bandwidth decisions use per-user /proc/PID/io byte rates.
When either IOPS dimension is configured, ResMan reads the authoritative
user slice's io.stat rios/wios counters. Page-cache hits, pipe traffic, and
socket traffic therefore cannot trigger device-IOPS enforcement. The first
sample after observation begins, a slice is recreated, or the process policy
changes is unknown rather than zero. Available users remain a lower bound that
may prove activation, but one eligible user without a comparable baseline
blocks the global release decision. The activation rule is any-of across
configured dimensions and the release rule is all-of.
.PP
An unavailable /proc/PID/io sample for an enforceable I/O-eligible process is
unknown, not zero. Available rates remain a lower bound and may still prove an
activation threshold exceeded. Otherwise resman maintains the current state;
active limits are never released from an incomplete I/O sample. Current
coverage failures are exported through
.BR resman_procfs_unavailable_processes {access}.
.IP \(bu
.B IO_DEVICE_FILTER
- Device specification. "all" enumerates all whole block devices in /sys/block;
  alternatively specify one "major:minor" device.
.IP \(bu
.B IO_THRESHOLD_DURATION
- Seconds to wait before activating IO limits (0 = immediate)
.IP \(bu
.B IO_USER_INCLUDE_LIST
- Regex patterns for users subject to IO limits (empty = all)
.IP \(bu
.B IO_USER_EXCLUDE_LIST
- Regex patterns for users excluded from IO limits
.SH SYSTEMD NATIVE ENFORCEMENT
On a host booted with systemd, ResMan uses
.B systemd_native
when the authoritative adapter is available. It applies runtime-only properties to
user.slice and user-UID.slice without moving PIDs out of their session or service.
The finite CPU parent indirectly bounds excluded users and rootless descendants.
RAM and I/O require complete independent authority; authority_split or
runtime_owned_descendant refuses them and releases their owned properties while CPU
may remain active. The durable systemd-property-leases.json journal is not a cache.
See /usr/share/doc/resman/SYSTEMD-PROPERTY-LEASES.md for guarded restoration and
operator-conflict recovery. Never delete this journal during a metrics reset.
.PP
If the adapter cannot establish authority, ResMan remains in
.B observation_only
mode without a fallback. This includes non-systemd hosts. Observation-only mode
and typed resource refusals are not enforcement. ResMan never creates its own
enforcement hierarchy, records process origins, or writes process membership.
.PP
Inspect
.B resman_enforcement_mode
or the MCP system and limits status before assuming that enforcement is available.
There is no configuration override for the systemd containment decision.
.SH PSI EVENT-DRIVEN MODE
When enabled, the daemon uses Linux Pressure Stall Information (PSI) files to
trigger control cycles when system pressure exceeds configured thresholds. The
fixed polling interval remains a fallback heartbeat.
.PP
PSI is not another CPU usage percentage. It measures stall/pressure: time in
which runnable tasks or IO operations are waiting because resources are
contended. For example, on a 4-core host, 4 CPU-bound threads can show 100% CPU
with little PSI if there is no meaningful run queue; more runnable threads than
available cores are more likely to generate CPU PSI.
.PP
The daemon opens
.B cpu.pressure
and
.B io.pressure
files from the cgroup root and uses the
.B poll()
syscall with
.B POLLPRI
to block until the kernel signals that pressure has exceeded the stall threshold.
Older CPU pressure files that expose only the
.B some
line are supported; the
.B full
line is optional.
When a PSI event fires, the daemon immediately runs a control cycle.
Control-cycle logs and Prometheus metrics include the trigger source, such as
.B initial ,
.B ticker ,
.B psi_system_cpu ,
.B psi_system_io ,
.B psi_user_cpu ,
or
.B psi_user_io .
.PP
A fallback polling interval (heartbeat) ensures control cycles still run if PSI
events are not generated for an extended period (e.g., when the system is idle).
This heartbeat is for the decision loop only. Prometheus/Grafana metrics are
refreshed separately using
.B METRICS_REFRESH_INTERVAL
in both polling and PSI modes, including blackout. Host observations remain current
without advancing decision baselines. Per-user series and SQLite history remain
decision-owned and are not sampled during blackout.
.PP
When a limited user experiences CPU throttling (pressure exceeds threshold in their
per-user cgroup), the daemon temporarily boosts their cpu.weight to grant more CPU
time within the shared quota (adaptive fairness). The boost reverts automatically
after the configured duration.
.PP
Configuration parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
.B PSI_EVENT_DRIVEN
- Enable PSI event-driven mode (true/false, default: false)
.PP
PSI mode, CPU and I/O stall thresholds, the tracking window, fallback interval,
and metrics refresh interval support configuration reload. Changes to the mode,
thresholds, or window rebuild the kernel PSI monitors. Loop interval changes
take effect immediately.
.IP \(bu
.B PSI_CPU_STALL_THRESHOLD
- CPU stall threshold in microseconds (default: 50000 = 5% on 1s window)
.IP \(bu
.B PSI_IO_STALL_THRESHOLD
- IO stall threshold in microseconds (default: 50000)
.IP \(bu
.B PSI_WINDOW_US
- PSI tracking window in microseconds (default: 1000000 = 1 second)
.IP \(bu
.B PSI_FALLBACK_INTERVAL
- Fallback polling interval in seconds when event-driven (default: 300 = 5min)
.IP \(bu
.B METRICS_REFRESH_INTERVAL
- Host Prometheus/Grafana refresh interval in seconds, including blackout (default: 30)
.PP
Requirements:
.IP \(bu 2
Linux kernel >= 4.20 with CONFIG_PSI=y
.IP \(bu
cgroups v2 with cpu.pressure and io.pressure files available
.IP \(bu
.B golang.org/x/sys
package (included as direct dependency)
.PP
Example configuration:
.sp
.nf
PSI_EVENT_DRIVEN=true
PSI_CPU_STALL_THRESHOLD=50000    # 5% CPU pressure
PSI_IO_STALL_THRESHOLD=50000     # 5% IO pressure
PSI_WINDOW_US=1000000            # 1 second window
PSI_FALLBACK_INTERVAL=300        # 5 minute heartbeat
METRICS_REFRESH_INTERVAL=30      # Dashboard refresh interval
.fi
.sp
.SH WORKLOAD PATTERN DETECTION
When enabled, the daemon analyzes historical CPU usage to classify eligible users
as "batch" or "interactive" and selects the corresponding RAM policy. CPU capacity
remains owned exclusively by CPU Points.
.PP
Pattern detection respects
.B USER_INCLUDE_LIST
and
.B USER_EXCLUDE_LIST
and never applies policy to an excluded user. Pattern RAM limits are applied to the
user's existing sub\-cgroup only while global limits are active; pattern analysis
does not move processes or change CPU weights.
.PP
When a classification becomes unknown, the user becomes ineligible, or
.B AUTODETECT_PATTERNS
is disabled, any active pattern policy is removed. A limited user retains its CPU
Points allocation and returns to the configured non-pattern RAM limit.
.PP
The pattern detection algorithm uses a sliding window of
.B PATTERN_HISTORY_HOURS
hours and requires at least
.B PATTERN_MIN_SAMPLES
distinct hourly buckets before making decisions. Classification confidence must exceed
.B PATTERN_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD
(0.0\-1.0).
.PP
Samples collected within the same wall\-clock hour are aggregated into one hourly bucket. Each hourly bucket has equal weight regardless of polling frequency or PSI events. Buckets older than
.B PATTERN_HISTORY_HOURS
are removed, including for continuously active users.
.PP
Detected patterns are observational classifications and do not mutate resource limits:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B Batch night
- Users with high resource usage during night hours.
.IP \(bu
.B Interactive day
- Users with moderate daytime usage.
.RE
.PP
Configuration parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
.B AUTODETECT_PATTERNS
- Enable automatic pattern detection (true/false)
.IP \(bu
.B PATTERN_HISTORY_HOURS
- Historical window in hours (default: 168 = 1 week)
.IP \(bu
.B PATTERN_MIN_SAMPLES
- Minimum number of distinct hourly buckets required for classification
.IP \(bu
.B PATTERN_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD
- Confidence threshold (0.0\-1.0)
.PP
Pattern detection runs hourly and updates observed user classifications automatically.
.SH CONTROL CYCLE
The daemon executes control cycles at regular intervals:
.IP 1. 3
Collects system metrics (total CPU usage, per\-user CPU, load average)
.IP 2.
Decision making based on configured thresholds
.IP 3.
Applying/removing limits via cgroups
.IP 4.
Logging and Prometheus metrics update (if enabled)
.SH PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATIONS
The daemon incorporates several performance optimizations to minimize overhead on managed systems:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B gopsutil integration
- Efficient process scanning via the proven gopsutil library, replacing custom /proc parsing.
.IP \(bu
.B sync.Map regex cache
- Thread\-safe caching of compiled regular expressions for user filtering, eliminating race conditions.
.IP \(bu
.B Consolidated /proc scans
- Single scan of /proc for all user metrics, reducing I/O operations by ~70%.
.IP \(bu
.B Sharded mutexes
- Fine\-grained locking for per\-process CPU tracking to minimize contention.
.IP \(bu
.B Pre\-allocated slices
- Estimated capacity allocation for dynamic slices to reduce heap allocations.
.IP \(bu
.B Per-entry cache retention
- Each metric value retains its owning TTL, while completed process scans prune exited PID baselines.
.RE
.PP
These optimizations ensure that monitoring overhead remains low even on systems with thousands of processes.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
The configuration file supports key=value format. Full\-line comments start with
\(aq#\(aq. An inline \(aq#\(aq starts a comment only outside quotes and when preceded by
whitespace.
Timeout parameters for cgroup operations, retry delays, and MCP shutdown can be configured to improve reliability in high\-load scenarios.
.PP
.B METRICS_CACHE_TTL
controls observation value reuse and must be at least one second. A value remains
valid at the exact TTL boundary and expires after it; periodic cleanup does not
shorten values above five minutes. Control-cycle host CPU decisions use an independent,
uncached /proc/stat baseline. CPU and I/O process baselines are also independent of
this TTL and remain valid for active PID and start-time identities until a completed
scan proves that the process disappeared.
.PP
The active configuration path is selected by the
.B \-\-config
command\-line option (default:
.IR /etc/resman/resman.conf ).
It cannot be redirected by a key inside the configuration file or by an
environment variable.
.PP
When the default path is selected, startup refuses any legacy
.I /etc/resman.conf
or RPM-saved
.I /etc/resman.conf.rpmsave
and the secret-bearing
.I /etc/resman.conf.backup
and
.I /etc/resman.conf.tmp
entries, plus every potential configuration copy matching
.IR /etc/resman.conf.backup_* ,
even when the new packaged configuration already exists. Stop resman,
choose the authoritative authored contents from the legacy or RPM-saved file,
install them as a regular file at
.IR /etc/resman/resman.conf ,
remove the legacy source files, move any needed operator-managed matching copies
to a protected archive outside the legacy path, securely remove generated or
unneeded matching copies and fixed-name orphaned artifacts, and restart. The
read-only startup guard deliberately treats every matching name as potentially
credential-bearing; automatic cleanup removes only exact historical generated
names. An explicitly selected custom configuration path is not treated as a
legacy fallback.
.PP
When metrics persistence is enabled at its default
.I /var/lib/resman/metrics.db
path, startup likewise refuses a legacy
.IR /etc/resman/metrics.db .
Earlier database schemas are incompatible: archive or delete the database
and restart so resman can create schema version 7. It is never migrated or opened
under the new path.
.PP
Configuration updates initiated through MCP require this path to be a regular file.
Symbolic links, including dangling links, are rejected without replacing the link or
changing its target. Select the target itself with
.B \-\-config
or replace the link with a regular file. Atomic replacement preserves the source mode
and ownership and fails before writing secret-bearing content if the service account
cannot apply the required owner. In that case, grant the service permission to change
file ownership or change the source ownership to the service account before retrying.
If a failed atomic replacement cannot restore the rolling backup, stop resman, restore
.IR /etc/resman/resman.conf.backup ,
and restart before accepting another configuration write; the active file and runtime
configuration may differ until recovery is complete. Resman rejects subsequent
configuration persistence attempts until restart. If a newly created configuration has
no rolling backup and its compensating removal fails, stop resman, remove the new file,
and restart.
.PP
.B Authoritative configuration reference
.PP
The complete public-key inventory is generated from the runtime configuration,
defaults, and lifecycle table. It is installed as
.IR /usr/share/doc/resman/CONFIGURATION.md .
.PP
.B Upgrade from 1.25.x through 1.34.1 to 1.35.2
.PP
ResMan 1.35.2 includes the intentional 1.34.0 filesystem, database,
configuration, MCP, Prometheus, hook, capability and container contracts. Before
installing it, follow
.IR /usr/share/doc/resman/UPGRADING.md .
It lists the visible symptom, cause and required action for every known
discontinuity; old schemas, keys and wire aliases are not preserved.
.PP
The copyable configuration remains
.IR /etc/resman/resman.conf .
.PP
The default Prometheus bind is
.IR 127.0.0.1 .
A bind to
.I 0.0.0.0
is a non-default remote exposure and requires TLS, authentication, and firewall
restrictions.
.B SYSTEM_UID_MAX
is normally derived from
.I /proc/sys/kernel/pid_max
at startup, with 60000 used only if that file cannot be read. An empty
.B USER_INCLUDE_LIST
makes nobody eligible for CPU limiting, while empty RAM and I/O include lists
select every non-excluded user.
.PP
Idle user release is based on the per-user CPU exponential moving average.
.B MIN_ACTIVE_TIME
protects the most recent active enforcement epoch across CPU and RAM or IO,
because global deactivation releases every resource together. It also acts as
the minimum hold time after an individual CPU user is first limited or re-added.
Re-adding a user does not reset the CPU enforcement epoch. A future per-resource
release model must define and test its own per-resource activation timestamps.
Dynamic RAM and IO enable or user-filter changes are reconciled against already
active user cgroups.
.PP
Database retention runs at startup and every 24 hours. File-backed databases
use incremental auto-vacuum so periodic cleanup does not run a blocking full
database vacuum. Existing databases using auto-vacuum mode NONE are migrated
once at startup; this first upgraded startup runs a full VACUUM and may require
additional time and temporary free disk space proportional to the database
size. Each collection cycle is committed as one transaction.
File-backed databases use WAL mode with a 5-second busy timeout, and timestamps
are stored and queried in UTC.
.PP
Schema version 7 commits one common sample epoch and interval boundary for every
system and user row. CPU Points history separates configured class and optional
guarantee from applied class and weight, raw cgroup diagnostics, nullable comparable
usage deltas, the online-CPU denominator, programmed parent quota, flat sibling use and
parent throttling. RAM history separately reports process-derived memory,
memory.current, applied state, complete or partial charge coverage, memory.high/max
limits, swap policy, and high/max/OOM/kill event deltas. A NULL counter delta means
that no comparable baseline exists after startup, a read failure, counter reset or
cgroup recreation; zero means a comparable counter did not increase. Lifecycle
distinguishes ineligible, eligible inactive, applied, failed, and released users.
A released state is emitted only for a user observed in that sample.
.PP
For a file-backed
.B METRICS_DB_PATH
the immediate parent must be a real directory owned by the ResMan process UID
with mode 0700. ResMan creates a missing parent with that mode, but refuses an
existing directory with different ownership or permissions. A pre-existing
database and its
.B -wal
and
.B -shm
sidecars must be regular, non-symbolic-link files owned by the same UID with
mode 0600. Correct ownership and permissions explicitly before restarting;
ResMan does not silently change operator-owned artifacts. The
.B METRICS_DB_PATH
ancestor chain must contain no symbolic links and no directory from which an
untrusted owner, group, or other user can replace the checked child. A sticky
temporary directory is accepted when it protects the process-owned child. The
.B :memory:
database is exempt. These checks occur before SQLite opens the path so a
permissive service umask cannot expose history files.
.nf

# PSI EVENT-DRIVEN MODE (Linux >= 4.20, CONFIG_PSI=y)
# Uses poll() on cpu.pressure/io.pressure to trigger control cycles
# only when system pressure exceeds thresholds. Reduces overhead
# when the system is idle by using a longer fallback interval.
# PSI_EVENT_DRIVEN=true
# PSI_CPU_STALL_THRESHOLD=50000    # Stall threshold in microseconds
# PSI_IO_STALL_THRESHOLD=50000     # IO stall threshold in microseconds
# PSI_WINDOW_US=1000000            # Tracking window (1 second)
# PSI_FALLBACK_INTERVAL=300        # Heartbeat interval (5 minutes)
# METRICS_REFRESH_INTERVAL=30      # Dashboard refresh interval

# MCP TOOLS FOR HISTORICAL METRICS (when METRICS_DB_ENABLED=true):
# - get_user_history: Historical CPU/RAM for a user
# - get_system_history: Historical system metrics
# - get_user_summary: Aggregated statistics (avg/min/max)
# - get_metrics_database_info: Database status and info
.sp
.fi
.PP
A complete example configuration file with all available parameters can be found in the distribution as
.I config/resman.conf.example
.
.SS Activation Timing
.B CPU_THRESHOLD_DURATION
is not measured from process start. resman starts that timer only after a
control cycle measures per-user CPU above
.B CPU_THRESHOLD.
For a newly started CPU-bound process, the effective activation time is approximately:
.PP
.RS
.nf
one CPU delta sample + CPU_THRESHOLD_DURATION
.fi
.RE
.PP
With
.B POLLING_INTERVAL=30,
.B CPU_THRESHOLD_DURATION=90,
limits normally activate about 120 seconds after the first observation, plus
polling alignment. Every process state is included in instantaneous CPU deltas.
.B PROCESS_MIN_AGE_SECONDS
only stabilizes the lifetime-average CPU metric and does not delay activation.
.PP
With
.B IGNORE_SYSTEM_LOAD=false,
a high load average suppresses activation only when CPU-eligible users account
for less than half of measured aggregate host CPU activity. Host CPU is converted
from normalized 0-100 percentage into aggregate per-core percentage before the
comparison. An exact half remains actionable. If the host CPU sample is unavailable,
activation is delayed without discarding threshold-duration progress or claiming an
unmeasured external cause. The first control-cycle sample establishes the independent
/proc/stat baseline and is unavailable; a measured zero after that baseline remains a
valid value. Observation refreshes and METRICS_CACHE_TTL do not advance this baseline.
.PP
Global deactivation requires every actively limited user's CPU EMA to remain below
.B CPU_RELEASE_THRESHOLD
for three
.B POLLING_INTERVAL
periods. This cool-down uses elapsed wall-clock time, so additional control
cycles triggered by PSI events cannot shorten it.
.PP
.B total_cpu_usage
is the last valid host-wide normalized CPU percentage (0-100). The
.B total_cpu_usage_available
field says whether the latest observation refresh produced that value, while
.B total_cpu_usage_unavailable_reason
contains a bounded reason when it did not. An unavailable refresh retains the last
valid value instead of representing missing data as zero. Activation decisions use
per-user CPU usage
.RB ( cpu_eligible_users_cpu_usage ),
which is the sum of process CPU and can exceed 100 on multi-core systems.
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
All configuration options can be overridden by environment variables
with the same name. Example:
.sp
.nf
LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG resman \-\-config /etc/resman/resman.conf
.fi
.SH LOG FILES
.I /var/log/resman.log
.br
The log file is automatically rotated when it reaches the maximum size
configured in
.B LOG_MAX_SIZE,
which must be greater than zero.
Rotation creates a backup file with .1 extension.
New log files are created mode 0600. When an existing regular log is opened,
ResMan preserves only its owner/group read-write bits and removes all execute bits
and all permissions for other users. The rotated backup and replacement inherit
that sanitized access mode and ownership. The managed path must be a regular file;
symbolic links are rejected without changing their target. Packaged rsyslog and
logrotate defaults use 0600.
.PP
File and syslog writes, rotation steps, and sink close operations are checked. If the
configured sink fails, ResMan writes a bounded diagnostic directly to standard error
without recursively calling the failed logger. The failed record itself is not copied
to the fallback because it can contain sensitive context.
A later successful write marks the primary sink healthy again while retaining
cumulative and last-failure diagnostics. A control cycle whose completion record
cannot be written finishes its protective work and returns a degraded result instead
of claiming a successful cycle log. Ordinary diagnostic call sites use the same
health and fallback contract without changing their owning operation's result.
.SH STATE FILES
.I /var/lib/resman/systemd-property-leases.json
.br
Stores the private, root-only journal used to recover exact systemd property
ownership after interruption. It never records process membership or origins.
.SH PROMETHEUS METRICS
When enabled, the daemon exposes metrics at the configured
.B /metrics
endpoint over HTTP or HTTPS.
.IP \(bu 2
resman_cpu_total_usage_percent \- Total CPU usage
.IP \(bu
resman_control_cycle_host_cpu_sample_available \- Latest decision host CPU sample availability
.IP \(bu
resman_control_cycle_host_cpu_sample_unavailable_total \- Unavailable decision host CPU samples by bounded reason
.IP \(bu
resman_observation_host_cpu_sample_available \- Latest observation host CPU sample availability
.IP \(bu
resman_observation_host_cpu_sample_unavailable_total \- Unavailable observation host CPU samples by bounded reason
.IP \(bu
resman_all_users_cpu_usage_percent \- Non\-system user CPU usage
.IP \(bu
resman_memory_usage_megabytes \- Total system memory usage
.IP \(bu
