resman_enforcement_mode{mode} \- Current bounded enforcement mode: observation_only or systemd_native (gauge)
.IP \(bu
resman_procfs_unavailable_processes{access} \- Current processes missing executable_identity or io_decision procfs access (gauge)
.PP
All user-specific metrics include
.B uid
and
.B username
labels for easy filtering and aggregation in Grafana dashboards.
The
.B resman_user_cpu_limit_active
gauge reports observed CPU enforcement state: 1 means the user is in the
CPU-limited cgroup, while 0 means CPU enforcement is not observed.
.PP
Example queries:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Sum of all user memory:
.I sum(resman_user_memory_usage_bytes)
.IP \(bu
Top 5 users by CPU:
.I topk(5, resman_user_cpu_usage_percent)
.IP \(bu
Processes per user:
.I resman_user_process_count{username="francesco"}
.IP \(bu
Only limited users:
.I resman_user_cpu_limit_active == 1
.RE
.SH MCP SERVER (MODEL CONTEXT PROTOCOL)
When enabled, the daemon exposes an MCP server for AI assistant integration.
The MCP server provides tools and resources for querying system status and generating reports.
.PP
ResMan accepts only MCP revision 2026-07-28 over stdio or HTTP. The protocol is
stateless: every request is self-describing, HTTP authentication is evaluated
per request, and no MCP session identifier or initialization exchange is
supported. Pre-2026 revisions and compatibility fallbacks are rejected.
.PP
MCP over HTTP is HTTPS-only and requires
.B MCP_TLS_ENABLED=true,
a server certificate, and a key. The default MCP certificate and key paths are
the same literal paths used by Prometheus, but the MCP and Prometheus settings
are independent. The default minimum version is TLS 1.3. Setting
.B MCP_TLS_CA_FILE
enables mutual TLS and makes a client certificate mandatory in addition to the
per-request bearer token. The stdio transport does not require certificates.
.PP
Available MCP tools (this list is verified against the production discovery response):
.\" BEGIN MCP TOOL INVENTORY
.IP \(bu 2
.B get_system_status
\- Current CPU/memory status with hostname
.IP \(bu
.B get_user_metrics
\- Per-user CPU, memory, and process metrics
.IP \(bu
.B get_active_users
\- List of active non-system users
.IP \(bu
.B get_limits_status
\- CPU limits status and details
.IP \(bu
.B get_configuration
\- Current daemon configuration
.IP \(bu
.B get_configuration_editor
\- Redacted versioned configuration-editor snapshot
.IP \(bu
.B update_configuration
\- Revision-bound partial configuration update (requires MCP_ALLOW_WRITE_OPS=true)
.IP \(bu
.B update_cpu_points
\- Revision-bound CPU Points map update (requires MCP_ALLOW_WRITE_OPS=true)
.IP \(bu
.B get_control_history
\- Recent control cycle history
.IP \(bu
.B get_cpu_report
\- Comprehensive CPU usage report (formatted text)
.IP \(bu
.B get_mem_report
\- Comprehensive memory usage report (formatted text)
.IP \(bu
.B set_user_exclude_list
\- Persist and apply CPU exclusion patterns (requires MCP_ALLOW_WRITE_OPS=true)
.IP \(bu
.B set_user_include_list
\- Persist and apply CPU eligibility patterns (requires MCP_ALLOW_WRITE_OPS=true)
.IP \(bu
.B get_user_filters
\- Get current CPU include and exclude patterns
.IP \(bu
.B validate_user_filter_pattern
\- Validate a user-filter regular expression
.IP \(bu
.B activate_limits
\- Manually activate CPU limits (requires MCP_ALLOW_WRITE_OPS=true)
.IP \(bu
.B deactivate_limits
- Manually deactivate limits (requires MCP_ALLOW_WRITE_OPS=true)
.IP \(bu
.B get_user_history
- Historical CPU/RAM metrics for a specific user (requires METRICS_DB_ENABLED=true)
.IP \(bu
.B get_system_history
- Historical system metrics (requires METRICS_DB_ENABLED=true)
.IP \(bu
.B get_user_summary
- Aggregated statistics (avg/min/max) for a user (requires METRICS_DB_ENABLED=true)
.IP \(bu
.B get_metrics_database_info
- Database status and information (requires METRICS_DB_ENABLED=true)
.\" END MCP TOOL INVENTORY
.PP
System-wide payloads and the shared active-user and configuration schemas include a
.B hostname
field for multi-server environment identification. Per-user entries are identified by
uid and username inside those host-scoped responses.
.PP
Fixed MCP resources (this list is verified against the production discovery response):
.\" BEGIN MCP FIXED RESOURCE INVENTORY
.IP \(bu 2
.B resman://system/status
\- Real-time system status
.IP \(bu
.B resman://users/active
\- List of active users
.IP \(bu
.B resman://limits/status
\- Current limits status
.IP \(bu
.B resman://config
\- Current configuration
.\" END MCP FIXED RESOURCE INVENTORY
.PP
MCP resource URI templates (this list is verified against the production discovery response):
.\" BEGIN MCP RESOURCE TEMPLATE INVENTORY
.IP \(bu
.B resman://users/{uid}/metrics
\- Per-user metrics
.\" END MCP RESOURCE TEMPLATE INVENTORY
.PP
MCP prompts (this list is verified against the production discovery response):
.\" BEGIN MCP PROMPT INVENTORY
.IP \(bu 2
.B system-health
\- Quick system health check with assessment
.IP \(bu
.B user-analysis
\- Analyze resource usage by user in table format
.IP \(bu
.B troubleshooting
\- Diagnose CPU limit issues
.\" END MCP PROMPT INVENTORY
.PP
The
.B get_active_users
tool and
.B resman://users/active
resource share one object schema with hostname, server_role, and user entries containing
both uid and username. The
.B get_configuration
tool and
.B resman://config
resource share one resource-policy schema covering CPU, RAM, and I/O settings plus host
identity. The payload deliberately excludes credentials and settings outside that scope.
The per-user metrics tool and resource likewise share one typed projection. Historical
user and system records use distinct schemas because their fields have different meanings.
.PP
Example MCP configuration:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Enable MCP:
.I MCP_ENABLED=true
.IP \(bu
Transport mode:
.I MCP_TRANSPORT=stdio
(or
.IR http )
.IP \(bu
HTTP port (for http transport):
.I MCP_HTTP_PORT=1969
.IP \(bu
TLS certificate and key (mandatory for http transport):
.I MCP_TLS_CERT_FILE=/etc/resman/tls/server.crt
and
.I MCP_TLS_KEY_FILE=/etc/resman/tls/server.key
.IP \(bu
Allow write operations:
.I MCP_ALLOW_WRITE_OPS=false
.IP \(bu
Full operator credential for HTTP:
.I MCP_AUTH_TOKEN
.IP \(bu
Distinct least-privilege editor credential required with HTTP writes:
.I MCP_EDITOR_AUTH_TOKEN
.RE
.PP
The operator credential retains every enabled MCP tool. The editor credential is
restricted by a positive allowlist to configuration-editor operations and observed-state
reads; it cannot activate or deactivate limits, use the user-filter setters, or gain
access to future tools by default. Editor reads are redacted and return one composite
revision for the configuration and CPU Points files. Partial updates preserve omitted
values, comments, ownership, mode, and secret bytes, then return exactly one terminal
state: applied, pending_restart, refused, or failed.
.SH LIMIT HOOKS
When
.B LIMIT_HOOK_ENABLED
is true, ResMan can run an external script, call a webservice, or both whenever
a user is newly limited.
.PP
Script hooks are configured with
.B LIMIT_HOOK_SCRIPT
and require restart-only
.B LIMIT_HOOK_SCRIPT_USER
and
.BR LIMIT_HOOK_SCRIPT_GROUP .
The exact NSS user and group must be non-root. They are rejected when no script is
configured. The script path must be absolute, regular, free of symbolic-link
components, executable by that identity, and have no untrusted writable ancestor.
ResMan starts it with that UID and GID, clears supplementary groups, sets the working
directory to /, and supplies only PATH, LANG, LC_ALL, HOME, PWD, USER, LOGNAME, and
the following event variables:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
RESMAN_LIMIT_UID
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_USERNAME
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_ENFORCEABLE_CPU_USAGE_PERCENT
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_ELIGIBLE_USERS_COUNT
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_TIMESTAMP
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_SERVER_ROLE
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_CONFIGURED_CLASS
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_CONFIGURED_GUARANTEE
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_LIFECYCLE_STATE
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_APPLIED_CLASS
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_APPLIED_WEIGHT
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_APPLIED_TO_PROCESSES
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_COMPLETE_UID_WORKLOAD_GUARANTEED
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_RECONCILIATION_DEGRADED
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_PROCESS_COVERAGE
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_RAM_CGROUP_MEMORY_CURRENT_BYTES
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_RAM_COVERAGE
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_RAM_COVERAGE_INCOMPLETE_PROCESS_COUNT
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_RAM_SWAP_DISABLED
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_HIGH
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_MAX
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_SWAP_MAX
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_HIGH_EVENTS_DELTA
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_MAX_EVENTS_DELTA
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_OOM_EVENTS_DELTA
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_OOM_KILL_EVENTS_DELTA
.RE
.PP
Webservice hooks are configured with
.B LIMIT_HOOK_URL
and receive an HTTP POST with a JSON payload containing uid, username,
enforceable_cpu_usage_percent, cpu_eligible_users_count, timestamp, source,
server_role, the typed CPU Points configured/applied lifecycle, bounded process
coverage, and authoritative systemd-slice RAM
coverage. RAM limits and interval `memory.high`, `memory.max`, OOM, and OOM-kill deltas
remain distinct; growing high events with zero max/OOM/kill events can describe supported
throttling or stall and does not imply absent enforcement. An absent mapped guarantee is
not zero, and a partial host subset is never reported as the complete UID workload.
.PP
Hook script stdout and stderr are discarded and are never included in returned
errors or daemon logs. Script failures retain a bounded reason such as timeout or
exit status. Webservice failure logs identify only the URL scheme and host (including
an explicit port); user information, path, query values, and fragments are omitted.
.PP
Hook execution uses a fixed worker pool selected by restart-only
.B LIMIT_HOOK_MAX_CONCURRENCY
and a fixed pending-delivery queue selected by restart-only
.BR LIMIT_HOOK_QUEUE_CAPACITY .
Admission never blocks enforcement. A full queue rejects the delivery without retry
or durable spooling, records the terminal saturated outcome, and emits a rate-limited
bounded warning.
.PP
Script and HTTP are separate jobs. Each receives its own complete
.B LIMIT_HOOK_TIMEOUT
deadline, so one mechanism never consumes the other's budget. HTTP uses a dedicated
client with bounded phases and no retry. Every accepted delivery records one terminal
success, failure, timeout, or cancellation outcome; a rejected admission records
saturated. On shutdown ResMan rejects new hook dispatch, cancels queued and in-flight
hooks, and drains the workers before continuing cleanup. The control cycle does not
wait for ordinary hook completion.
.PP
Scripts run in their own process group. Timeout and shutdown send TERM, escalate to
KILL after a bounded grace period, and verify that the group has drained. This bounds
ordinary descendants but does not claim hard containment of a malicious process that
deliberately escapes the group; such containment requires a separate systemd
transient-unit or cgroup TasksMax design. See docs/LIMIT-HOOKS.md in the source and
documentation package for operational details.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGHUP
\- Reload configuration
.PP
Runtime thresholds, filters, PSI settings, polling intervals, and
.B LOG_LEVEL
are applied dynamically. This includes
.BR USERNAME_CACHE_TTL
regardless of whether the metrics database is enabled, and
.BR METRICS_DB_RETENTION_DAYS .
The cgroup observation root, Prometheus listener and security settings,
.BR LOG_FILE ,
.BR LOG_MAX_SIZE ,
.BR USE_SYSLOG ,
metrics database enablement, path and write interval, server role, and MCP
enablement, transport, listener, authentication, and write permissions
require a service restart. Requested changes to static fields are logged and
rejected with an error naming each key; their previous effective values remain
active. Restart the service to apply those keys. Configuration values and
credentials are not included in that error.
.PP
Reload is published as one configuration epoch. Control cycles and metrics
refreshes finish entirely on the old epoch or wait until all runtime consumers
hold the new epoch; they never observe a partial cross-component update.
.br
.B SIGINT, SIGTERM
\- Graceful shutdown
.SH EXAMPLES
Start with custom configuration:
.sp
.nf
resman \-\-config /etc/my\-config.conf
.fi
.sp
Start with debug logging:
.sp
.nf
LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG resman
.fi
.sp
Check version:
.sp
.nf
resman \-\-version
.fi
